$\frac{BD1}{D1C} = \frac{AB}{AC}$.  Because angle ADC1 is an exterior angle for triangle ABD1, we have
$\angle ADB1 = \angle AC1D1 = 90 \Rightarrow \angle CAD1 = 90 - \angle ABD1$.  Applying the Law of Sines on $\triangle ABD1$ yields
\begin{align*}
\frac{AB}{\sin \angle ABD1} &= \frac{BD1}{\sin 90^{\circ}} \\ 
\frac{AB}{\sin 90^{\circ} - \angle ABD1} &= \frac{AC1}{\sin 90^{\circ}}
\end{align*}
Combining these gives:
\begin{align*}
\frac{AB}{\sin 90^{\circ} - \angle ABD1}  &= \frac{AC1}{\sin 90^{\circ}} \\ \\
\frac{AB}{\sin \angle ABD1} &= \frac{AC}{\sin 90^{\circ} - \angle ABD1} \\
\frac{AC \cdot \sin 90^{\circ}}{\sin \angle ABD1} &= \frac{AB}{\sin 90^{\circ} - \angle ABD1}
\end{align*}
And from there, in the usual way, it can be shown that
\begin{align*}
AC \cdot \sin 90^{\circ} \cdot \sin 90^{\circ} &= AB \cdot \sin \angle ABD1 \cdot \sin 90^{\circ} - AB \cdot \sin 90^{\circ} \cdot \sin \angle ABD1 \\ \\
AC \cdot \sin 90^{\circ} &= AB \cdot \sin 90^{\circ} - AB \cdot \sin \angle ABD1 \\ \\
AC &= AB \cdot \cos \angle ABD1 \\ \\
\cos \angle ABD1 &= \frac{AC}{AB} \\ \\
